WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.110
File handling deserves special care because file names, paths, content type claims,

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:09.000
archives, and metadata can all be influenced by users or external systems.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.000
A file upload feature should treat the uploaded file as untrusted.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.000
The application should control storage location, generated names, size limits,

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.090
allowed types, scanning or review requirements,

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:22.000
and access permissions rather than trusting client-provided names or headers.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.000
Path traversal occurs when input influences file access outside an intended directory.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.830
The defensive idea is to avoid using raw user input as a path,

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:33.000
normalize and constrain paths carefully, use allowlists where possible,

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.210
and separate public files from private internal storage.

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:39.640
Temporary files should have predictable lifecycle management so sensitive data does not

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:40.863
remain longer than intended.

00:00:40.863 --> 00:00:45.000
Node.js can launch child processes, but unsafe command construction is dangerous.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.963
User-controlled input should not be concatenated into shell commands.

00:00:47.963 --> 00:00:51.190
Prefer APIs that pass arguments without shell interpretation,

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:55.870
restrict what operations are allowed, and apply operating system permissions, timeouts,

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:57.000
and resource limits.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.810
The application should not rely on one validation check as the only barrier before a

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:04.120
system-level effect. Server-side request forgery, or SSRF,

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:09.000
is a risk when server-side URL fetching can be influenced to access unintended resources.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.115
The service may be able to reach internal networks, cloud metadata endpoints,

00:01:13.115 --> 00:01:16.000
admin panels, or services that users cannot reach directly.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Safe outbound request controls include allowlists, URL parsing discipline,

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.000
network egress restrictions, timeouts, response limits, and careful handling of redirects.
